    "body": "## Overview\n\nPass the existing GPU block-table slice to FlashInfer's non-DCP prefill `plan()`. Without it, a backend that consumes rectangular block tables reconstructs the same table from CSR page indices during planning.\n\n## Claims\n\n- Avoid redundant block-table preparation using the existing public `block_tables` argument, which is already supported by the pinned FlashInfer 0.7.2.\n- Keep the prefill rows aligned when a batch also contains decode requests. This follows the existing DCP caller pattern, with an explicit `num_reqs` endpoint.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Changed-file `pre-commit` passed, including Ruff and mypy; `git diff --check` passed.\n- A separate GPU harness passed 64 public-wrapper cases across eight fresh processes: FP64-reference O and requested cuDNN LSE, nonzero CSR prefix offsets, unused table entries, table updates, fresh bindings, replan and retained CUDA Graph replay. FA2 fallback cases checked O. The table-reconstruction helper was called with the omitted argument and bypassed with the supplied table.\n- Qwen3-8B HTTP serving passed 960 requests across four fresh old/new servers, followed by 1,440 requests in two servers alternating the exact old/new metadata-builder body between completed cohorts. Full prefill and cached-prefix workloads used concurrency 1 and 4. Expected cache-hit counts and one-token completion semantics passed; all 22 distinct prompt groups returned the same greedy token. This is a targeted one-token model check, not a general model-quality evaluation.\n\nServing used vLLM `2d40c0609f`, whose entire affected Python module is byte-identical to this PR's base `be2de71198`, and a development FlashInfer/cuDNN stack to exercise the cuDNN path. It was not a full native rebuild of the new base. Stock FlashInfer 0.7.2 was checked for argument compatibility; these results do not imply that its default selector chooses cuDNN.\n\nThe serving checks above validate execution and request semantics. No serving performance result is included in this PR.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Validation commands and workload</summary>\n\n```bash\n.venv/bin/pre-commit run --files vllm/v1/attention/backends/flashinfer.py\ngit diff --check\n```\n\nThe serving harness started the OpenAI server with Qwen3-8B, BF16, `--max-model-len 16384`, `--max-num-batched-tokens 8192`, `--max-num-seqs 256`, prefix caching, 24 GiB KV storage, and `--attention-config '{\"backend\":\"FLASHINFER\",\"use_trtllm_attention\":false}'`. Requests used temperature 0 and one output token. Full prompts were 1,024 tokens; mixed cached-prefix requests had Q/KV lengths 128/8,320 and 512/2,560. Separate observed cohorts verified the actual provider and the table passed to public `plan()`; ordinary timing cohorts removed the observers. The development harnesses are not added as production tests or timing goldens.\n\n</details>\n\n## Details\n\n`common_attn_metadata.block_table_tensor` already contains the required GPU table. The added argument passes a row view without copying its contents. CSR metadata remains available to FlashInfer implementations that use it. This adds no API, cache, synchronization, dispatch rule, or per-layer work.\n\nChecked open PRs for FlashInfer prefill and block-table work. [#58519](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/58519) concerns DCP LSE/host preparation, and [#59493](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/59493) protects planning staging-buffer lifetime; neither forwards this non-DCP prefill table. No matching block-table forwarding PR was found.\n\nAI assistance was used for source inspection, implementation, validation and this description.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Reuses an existing argument and GPU table; one production line.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Public-wrapper